Identify the people and other subjects described by the statements. Use the statements and source context
to distinguish who did what. Use supporting_evidence to cite claim IDs describing a subject and participant IDs identifying that speaker. A cited participant ID binds that speaker to this identity.
Subjects mentioned by a speaker can be different from that speaker.

Choose existing when the evidence identifies a supplied registry entry, and return its entity_id. Set title to a new name only when the evidence establishes a name change; otherwise null.
Choose new for a distinct subject without an established registry match, giving a title and entity_type.
Choose review_required when identity is uncertain: describe the unresolved person or thing with a neutral title,
and list plausible registry IDs in candidate_entity_ids. Different possible identities of one unknown subject
belong in one entry; explicitly distinct subjects need separate entries.

Combine evidence for an established identity into one entry. Include concrete people and ongoing projects described
in claims even if they are not speakers. Incidental objects need not become separate subjects.
Preserve the difference between a project and the person working on it. A subject can be known before it has a page.

A required user object represents the explicitly bound You. Put that person's evidence there; subjects contains
other identities. External speakers are themselves, not You. Registry IDs and human-reviewed identities are
authoritative. Include alternate names in aliases. The application assigns new IDs and decides pages later.
